{"schema":1,"id":"git-branches","title":"Why does a merge sometimes need a new commit?","lang":"en","sources":["https://git-scm.com/docs/git-merge/2.52.0","https://git-scm.com/docs/git-branch/2.52.0","https://learngitbranching.js.org/"],"settings":[{"id":"history","label":"Main history","default":"diverged","options":[{"value":"diverged","label":"Main makes its own commit"},{"value":"linear","label":"Main stays at the shared commit"}]}],"answer":"A merge needs a new commit when each branch has a commit the other lacks","checks":[{"question":"After M is made, stay on main and run git merge feature again. Does main move back to B, make another commit, or stay at M?","answer":"It stays at M. B is already in M’s history, so Git reports Already up to date. Main has already incorporated feature; moving main back to B would discard C from main’s history."},{"question":"After M is made, switch to feature and run git merge main, with fast-forwards allowed. New commit, fast-forward, or no change?","answer":"Fast-forward. Feature is at B, which is already one of M’s parents, while main has commits feature lacks. Git moves feature from B to M without making a commit."},{"question":"Start again with feature at B and main at M. On feature, run git merge --no-ff main. Could Git fast-forward, and what does this option ask it to do instead?","answer":"A fast-forward would be possible because B is in M’s history. --no-ff asks Git to create a merge commit anyway, with B as its first parent and M as its second. The history makes a fast-forward possible; the chosen merge policy decides whether to use it."}],"scenarios":[{"values":{"history":"diverged"},"name":"Main history: Main makes its own commit","duration":83.267,"paper":"#ffffff","steps":[{"step":"question","title":"Both branches moved on. Can a merge just move main to B?","line":"Predict: if the main label moved to B, which edit would main lose?","caption":"Both branches started at A, where colour.txt is blue. On feature, B made it green; on main, commit C added title.txt, Home. We have main checked out (HEAD), so the files below are C’s. A branch label names a commit; arrows lead back through its history. Could moving main to B combine the edits?","start":0,"end":20.267,"hold":0.533,"state":{"frame":{"commits":[{"id":"A","parents":[],"colour":"blue","title":null},{"id":"B","parents":["A"],"colour":"green","title":null},{"id":"C","parents":["A"],"colour":"blue","title":"Home"}],"main":"C","feature":"B","head":"main","current":"C","merge":"none"},"files":{"colour":"blue","title":"Home"},"advanced":true},"changed":{"frame":{"after":{"commits":[{"id":"A","parents":[],"colour":"blue","title":null},{"id":"B","parents":["A"],"colour":"green","title":null},{"id":"C","parents":["A"],"colour":"blue","title":"Home"}],"main":"C","feature":"B","head":"main","current":"C","merge":"none"}},"files":{"after":{"colour":"blue","title":"Home"}},"advanced":{"after":true}},"picture":"A. B. C. feature. main. HEAD. File. Working contents. colour.txt. blue. title.txt. Home. Arrows point from a commit to its parent.","drawing":"┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│                                         ┌────────────────┐\n│                                         │    feature     │\n│                                         └────────────────┘\n│                                              ╭─────╮\n│                                        ───────  B  │\n│            ╭─────╮       ──────────────      │     │\n│            │  A  │◀──────                    ╰─────╯\n│            │     │◀──────                    ╭─────╮\n│            ╰─────╯       ──────────────      │     │ ┌────────────┐\n│                                        ───────  C  │ │   main     ◀────   HEAD\n│                                              ╰─────╯ └────────────┘\n│\n│\n│                    File                             Working contents\n│                   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n│                      colour.txt               blue\n│\n│\n│                     title.txt                   Home\n│\n│                           Arrows point from a commit to its parent.\n│"},{"step":"lost","title":"Answer: the Home edit. No branch would reach C","line":"B’s history is B, then A; C is not in it.","caption":"With main on B, checking out main gives green and no title.txt. B’s history is B, then A, so neither branch would contain C or the Home edit. C is still stored: moving the label does not delete the commit. To keep main’s history, following parent arrows from its new destination must still reach its old commit. A merge needs a new commit when each branch has a commit the other lacks.","start":20.267,"end":47.267,"hold":20.967,"state":{"frame":{"commits":[{"id":"A","parents":[],"colour":"blue","title":null},{"id":"B","parents":["A"],"colour":"green","title":null},{"id":"C","parents":["A"],"colour":"blue","title":"Home"}],"main":"B","feature":"B","head":"main","current":"B","merge":"none"},"files":{"colour":"green","title":null},"advanced":true,"lost":["C"]},"changed":{"frame":{"before":{"commits":[{"id":"A","parents":[],"colour":"blue","title":null},{"id":"B","parents":["A"],"colour":"green","title":null},{"id":"C","parents":["A"],"colour":"blue","title":"Home"}],"main":"C","feature":"B","head":"main","current":"C","merge":"none"},"after":{"commits":[{"id":"A","parents":[],"colour":"blue","title":null},{"id":"B","parents":["A"],"colour":"green","title":null},{"id":"C","parents":["A"],"colour":"blue","title":"Home"}],"main":"B","feature":"B","head":"main","current":"B","merge":"none"}},"files":{"before":{"colour":"blue","title":"Home"},"after":{"colour":"green","title":null}},"lost":{"after":["C"]}},"picture":"A. B. C. feature. main. no branch. HEAD. File. Working contents. colour.txt. green. title.txt. absent. C is outside both branch histories.","drawing":"┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│                                   ┌────────────────┐┌────────────┐\n│                                   │    feature     ││   main     ◀────   HEAD\n│                                   └────────────────┘└────────────┘\n│                                               ╭─────╮\n│                                         ───────  B  │\n│             ╭─────╮       ──────────────      │     │\n│             │  A  │◀──────                    ╰─────╯\n│             │     │◀──────                    ╭─────╮\n│             ╰─────╯       ──────────────      │     │ ┌─────────────────────┐\n│                                         ───────  C  │ │      no branch      │\n│                                               ╰─────╯ └─────────────────────┘\n│\n│\n│                    File                             Working contents\n│                   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n│                      colour.txt                green\n│\n│\n│                     title.txt                  absent\n│\n│                              C is outside both branch histories.\n│"},{"step":"merge","title":"So git merge writes M, a commit with two parents","line":"Predict: if main had never made C, would Git still need M?","caption":"git merge feature creates M with parents C and B and moves main to it. M’s files have both edits, green and Home, because the two edits touch different files. Feature stays at B, and C stays in main’s history.","start":47.267,"end":62.267,"hold":47.967,"state":{"frame":{"commits":[{"id":"A","parents":[],"colour":"blue","title":null},{"id":"B","parents":["A"],"colour":"green","title":null},{"id":"C","parents":["A"],"colour":"blue","title":"Home"},{"id":"M","parents":["C","B"],"colour":"green","title":"Home"}],"main":"M","feature":"B","head":"main","current":"M","merge":"commit"},"files":{"colour":"green","title":"Home"},"advanced":true},"changed":{"frame":{"before